## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the practical application of WorkManager in a beginner-friendly offline-sync scenario using Room and Retrofit, emphasizing real-world constraints such as battery efficiency, network awareness, and data consistency.

## Key Concepts

- **WorkManager**: Constraints (charging, unmetered network) and periodic work scheduling
- **Room**: Upsert patterns and DAO design for idempotent operations
- **Retrofit**: Network fetching and model mapping
- **Repository Pattern**: Bridges network and local data sources
- **Resilience**: Retry policies and exponential backoff for handling transient failures

## Code Example

```kotlin
// Note Entity
@Entity
data class Note(@PrimaryKey val id: String, val title: String, val content: String)

// DAO with Upsert
@Dao
interface NoteDao {
    @Insert(onConflict = OnConflictStrategy.REPLACE)
    suspend fun upsertAll(notes: List<Note>)
}

// Repository
class NoteRepository(private val dao: NoteDao, private val api: NotesService) {
    suspend fun syncNotes() {
        val notes = api.getNotes()
        dao.upsertAll(notes)
    }
}

// WorkManager Setup
val constraints = Constraints.Builder()
    .setRequiresCharging(true)
    .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.UNMETERED)
    .build()

val syncRequest = PeriodicWorkRequestBuilder<NoteSyncWorker>(
    15, TimeUnit.MINUTES)
    .setConstraints(constraints)
    .setBackoffCriteria(BackoffPolicy.EXPONENTIAL, 1, TimeUnit.MINUTES)
    .build()
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle schema migrations in this flow?
- How would you verify idempotence in the upsert logic?
- What strategies would you use to debug sync failures in production?

## RecyclerView Implementation

- Create item layout XML with TextViews for name and email
- Extend RecyclerView.Adapter with custom ViewHolder class
- Override three required methods: onCreateViewHolder, onBindViewHolder, getItemCount
- Use ViewHolder pattern to avoid repeated findViewById calls
- Set adapter and LayoutManager on RecyclerView in Activity/Fragment

## Key Components

- **ViewHolder**: Holds references to view components
- **Adapter**: Bridges data with RecyclerView
- **LayoutManager**: Positions items (LinearLayoutManager for lists)
- **Data Binding**: Optional but recommended for cleaner code

```kotlin
class UserAdapter(private val users: List<User>) : 
    RecyclerView.Adapter<UserAdapter.UserViewHolder>() {

    class UserViewHolder(view: View) : RecyclerView.ViewHolder(view) {
        val nameText: TextView = view.findViewById(R.id.name)
        val emailText: TextView = view.findViewById(R.id.email)
    }

    override fun onCreateViewHolder(parent: ViewGroup, viewType: Int): UserViewHolder {
        val view = LayoutInflater.from(parent.context)
            .inflate(R.layout.item_user, parent, false)
        return UserViewHolder(view)
    }

    override fun onBindViewHolder(holder: UserViewHolder, position: Int) {
        val user = users[position]
        holder.nameText.text = user.name
        holder.emailText.text = user.email
    }

    override fun getItemCount() = users.size
}
```

